# Upstream: giac 2.1.0 usual.cc asin and ln. asin(sin(a)) = +-(a-n*pi) and asin(cos(a)) through sin(pi/2-a) are rules for a real a, but asin took them for any a with a numeric value: asin(sin(2*i)) gave i*pi-2*i (Home 1.14159265359*i, right 2*i), and acos, which is pi/2-asin, gave acos(cos(ln(-pi))) = (1+i)*pi-ln(-pi), a real 1.99686276774 (right ln(-pi) = 1.14472988585+3.14159265359*i). ln(a^b) = b*ln(a) checked a > 0 but not b: ln(2^(10*i)) gave 10*i*ln(2), 6.9314718056*i (right 0.64828649842*i). The asin rules now need a real a (a numeric value with a zero imaginary part, or is_real); a complex a keeps asin (and acos) unevaluated, so evalf computes the principal value. ln(a^b) = b*ln(a) also needs is_real(b). A real a keeps its exact answer (asin(sin(2)) = pi-2, acos(cos(4)) = 2*pi-4, ln(2^x) = x*ln(2)).
